Osza Browser - Changelog
========================

Unreleased
---------
The visual refresh: one icon language everywhere. Security tools: the
start of the bundled security suite.

Added:
- Security tools (16): the copy-link action on the self-test page's
  sections - the security self-test joins the deep links. Each of the
  page's four parts (the protections snapshot, the live fingerprint
  checks, the noise tiles, the verify-on-a-real-site links) now
  carries its own "#" anchor on its heading, and a plain click on it
  copies that section's address to the clipboard - the "#" becomes a
  checkmark in the accent color while the address sits there, then
  says "#" again - so a single part of the self-test can be pointed
  at, pasted into a note or a bug report, shared or bookmarked.
  Everything else that reads an href keeps its own meaning: modified
  clicks, middle-click, the context menu's "Copy link address",
  bookmarks. The copy prefers the async Clipboard API
  (navigator.clipboard.writeText) and falls back to the legacy
  execCommand path when the API is missing or refuses, exactly like
  the diff page's anchors - on the very clipboard the previous
  release taught the browser to answer for its own pages. The
  address is osza:selftest?section=<id> (the ids: protections,
  fingerprint, noise, verify), and the URL shape exists once:
  SelfTestPage::pageUrl() builds it, sectionIdFromUrl() reads it
  back, the scheme handler hands the requested section to the page,
  and the page's own script spells the same shape for its anchors -
  the same contract the settings-diff page's deep links follow. A
  request asking for a section lands focused on it: marked,
  scrolled to, introduced by a note that names the section by its
  own heading; an address naming a section the page does not have
  is answered with a note over the whole page, everything shown
  from the top, never an error - which sections exist is the page's
  judgment, and the browser stays a pipe for the one value. The
  offline verifier for the page grew from 29 to 76 checks: the URL
  shape is pinned on all four sides (pageUrl(), sectionIdFromUrl(),
  the scheme handler, the page's own anchor builder), and the copy
  action and the landing run in the mocked DOM on every path they
  can take - the async copy, the refused-then-legacy copy, nothing
  copying at all, the double click appending its state once, the
  modified click left to the browser, the focused and the unknown
  section. A new autotest pins the URL helpers (the shapes, the
  trimming, the round trip, the verbatim pass-through of an unknown
  id) and the real page through the scheme handler: the four
  anchors carry the pageUrl() addresses, the focused section is
  marked and noted, the unknown one is answered.
- Security tools (15): the copy-link action on the diff page's
  section anchors, and the padlock menu's per-site entries join the
  deep links. The "#" anchor of a tool's section carried the URL
  that opens the diff focused there, but taking that address
  anywhere meant reading it out of a tooltip-less hover or the
  context menu. A plain click on the anchor now copies the deep link
  to the clipboard - the "#" becomes a checkmark in the accent color
  while the address sits there, then says "#" again - and everything
  else that reads an href keeps its own meaning: modified clicks,
  middle-click, the context menu's "Copy link address", bookmarks.
  When nothing can be copied the anchor says so in its title, and
  the context menu's way still works. The copy prefers the async
  Clipboard API (navigator.clipboard.writeText) and falls back to
  the legacy execCommand path when the API is missing or refuses,
  so the anchor works on every Qt WebEngine Osza runs on. The
  clipboard itself needed a look: the async API asks for a feature
  permission, and the HTML5 permission manager has no site-settings
  row for it - on the browser's own osza: pages the request would
  warn about an unknown feature and never be answered, leaving the
  page's promise pending. WebPage::featurePermissionRequested now
  answers the clipboard features for osza: origins: a sanitized
  write is granted (the same thing the legacy path already allows
  for internal pages), a clipboard read is denied - matching
  JavascriptCanPaste, which stays off for them - and before Qt 6.6,
  where the two are one combined feature that the writes arrive as,
  it is granted, the pages being the browser's own either way.
  Every other feature still goes through the permission manager.
  The padlock menu's per-site entries (HTTPS-Only mode, fingerprint
  protection) carry the same "diff" link the dashboard's rows do -
  the answer to "what would the recommended setting for this tool
  be?" one click away from the per-site state - and the landing is
  shared now: SettingsDiffPage::recommendedBaseline() resolves the
  baseline through the preset catalog in the one place, and
  SettingsDiffPage::openToolDiff() decides where a tool key lands
  (the focused diff, or the baselines overview when the request
  cannot be answered honestly - never an error page), with the
  dashboard's slot delegating to it, so the two can never drift
  apart. The links hide with their rows on the browser's own pages,
  exactly like the entries they belong to. The offline verifier grew
  from 70 to 111 checks: the URL shape is pinned on five sides now -
  the page's links, pageUrl(), the scheme handler, the dashboard's
  rows and the padlock's entries, all through the one resolver and
  the one shared landing - and the copy action runs in the mocked
  DOM on every path it can take (the async copy, the refused-then-
  legacy copy, nothing copying at all, the modified click left to
  the browser), with the clipboard policy of webpage.cpp pinned
  statically: the grant, the deny, the Qt 6.6 split, older Qt's
  combined feature. A new autotest pins the padlock's side: exactly
  the two per-site tools carry a link, each showing the pageUrl()
  address of the recommended baseline focused on its tool, each
  tooltip naming the baseline and the tool, external links off,
  hidden with the rows on the browser's own pages - and the shared
  landing, with a real window, opens the very address the link
  shows, trims whitespace around a key, answers an unknown key with
  the overview and is a no-op without a window.
- Security tools (14): the dashboard's per-tool rows link to their
  part of the recommended baseline's diff. The osza:settings-diff
  page answers a "tool" query item and the import flow links to it,
  but the security dashboard - the one place every tool lives as a
  row with its state and its quick switch - could not reach the diff
  of a single tool. Every protection row now carries a "diff" link
  beside its quick switch, and following it opens the recommended
  baseline's diff focused on exactly that tool's section - the same
  osza:settings-diff?preset=recommended-baseline&tool=<key> address
  the page's own "#" anchors and the import dialog's "view as page"
  link carry, built by the same SettingsDiffPage::pageUrl(), so the
  dashboard, the page and the import flow can never land in
  different places. The baseline is resolved through the preset
  catalog - the id the Preferences picker defaults to, the project's
  recommendation for everyday browsing, the natural referent for
  "what would the recommended setting for this tool be?" - and the
  row link is only built when it can be honest: the porter's
  toolKeys() must know the key (the same judgment the page applies
  to a "tool" query item) and the catalog must carry the baseline.
  The link is rich text with external links off - osza: URLs belong
  to the browser, never the OS - and it never opens anything itself:
  it hands the tool's stable key to the new openToolDiff() slot,
  which builds the address in the one place the URL shape exists and
  says where it goes in its tooltip, by the baseline's and the
  tool's own names. The slot answers anything it can be handed: a
  key that names no tool, or none at all, opens the baselines
  overview - where every baseline this Osza knows is listed - never
  an error page; whitespace around a key is trimmed, like the page
  trims its query items; and without a window it is a no-op,
  exactly like the dashboard's self-test shortcut. The offline
  verifier grew to 70 checks: the URL shape is now pinned on four
  sides - the page's links, pageUrl(), the scheme handler, and the
  dashboard's row links, which must go through pageUrl(), resolve
  the baseline through the catalog and build a link only for a key
  the porter knows. A new autotest pins the rest: exactly one link
  per tool the porter knows, each showing the pageUrl() address of
  the recommended baseline focused on its tool, each tooltip naming
  the baseline and the tool, external links off, the window-less
  slot a no-op - and with a real browser window, the opened tab's
  URL is the deep link itself, whitespace around a key is trimmed
  to it, and an unknown key opens the overview.
- Security tools (13): per-tool deep links, and the diff page inside
  the import flow. The osza:settings-diff page now answers a "tool"
  query item (osza:settings-diff?preset=recommended-baseline&tool=
  secureDns): the diff opens focused on that tool's section - marked,
  scrolled to, and introduced by a note - and every tool section
  carries its own "#" link whose address is exactly that URL, so a
  single tool's part of a baseline can be pointed at, copied,
  shared, or bookmarked from the section it lands on. The focus is
  computed from the same porter preview the page already renders,
  and every request is answered: a tool the baseline would change
  gets its marked section; a tool it would leave alone gets a note
  saying which of the three reasons applies (the section would be
  skipped, with the porter's reason; the profile already runs the
  tool exactly as the baseline wants; or the baseline does not
  configure the tool at all); a "tool" item naming no security tool
  is answered with a warning line over the full diff, never an error
  page. Whether a string names one of the tools is judged by the new
  SecuritySettingsPorter::toolKeys(), derived from buildPlans() -
  the same authority that validates and applies sections - so no
  second list can drift. The import flow and the page now meet: the
  import preview dialog is shown with open() (window-modal, not
  application-modal), so while the decision is pending its parent
  window - and with it the profile being previewed - stays
  input-blocked, exactly as previewed, and a file saved from a
  built-in preset with a matching checksum carries a "view as page"
  link: a matching checksum over the preset's own export is the
  guarantee that the osza:settings-diff page for that preset shows
  the very diff in the dialog, so the link promises exactly what it
  opens. The link follows the tree's selection tool by tool (it
  retargets to ?preset=<id>&tool=<key> and says so in its own
  wording), hands its URL to the caller through the new
  diffPageRequested signal - the dialog never opens anything itself
  - and the import flows open it in a browser window of its own
  (mApp->createWindow), readable beside the decision. Files that
  only name a preset, or carry no checksum or a mismatching one, get
  no link: the page would promise a diff it cannot honestly show.
  Both import entry points (the dashboard's Import / Export menu and
  Preferences) apply on acceptance of the modeless dialog through
  the same continuation as before - same validation, same
  ChecksumPolicy::Allow only for a confirmed "import anyway", same
  summary. The offline verifier for the page grew to 66 checks: the
  URL shape now pins the "&tool=" deep links on all sides (page
  links, pageUrl(), the scheme handler's toolIdFromUrl(), the
  porter's key judgment), the data and i18n contracts cover the
  focus answers, and the Node VM scenarios gained the focused diff
  (marked and scrolled section, note as text), the quiet focus, and
  the unknown tool over the full diff. The autotests pin the rest:
  the URL round trips, focusTool data for every tool of every
  baseline (note matching what the preview says), the unknown tool
  and the tool-without-preset shapes, the page through the real
  scheme handler (focus marking, deep-link hrefs, the warning
  banner), and the dialog's link - shown exactly for verifiable
  baseline files, retargeting with the selection, and emitting the
  URL it shows.
- The osza:settings-diff page: the import preview's diff, as a page -
  and imports that name the preset they came from say so. The
  dashboard's baseline menu ("Preview what a baseline would
  change...") and the preset picker's new "Preview..." button open an
  internal page that shows what a recommended baseline would change
  in this profile, computed live at request time through
  previewDocument() - the one validation the apply step and the
  preview both consume, so the page can never disagree with what
  applying the baseline would actually do, and it reads the current
  values through the same live-manager snapshot an export uses. The
  overview lists the three built-in baselines with the number of
  settings each would change here right now; a baseline's diff
  renders one section per changed tool and one row per changed
  field - the current value against the value with the baseline -
  plus the porter's one-line exception summaries ("Clears the
  exception for: ...") reused as-is through exceptionSummaryLines(),
  so the page, the import dialog and the offline --diff all say the
  same thing about the same change; the sections that would be
  skipped with their reasons; the tools the baseline does not
  mention; and the tools this profile already runs exactly as the
  baseline wants, named instead of left as an absence - every one of
  the seven sections is accounted for. An id that is not one of the
  built-ins is a dead end with the baselines linked back, never an
  error page. The page is deliberately a preview and nothing more:
  applying stays a confirmed step in the Qt UI (the dashboard's
  "Import / Export" menu or Preferences), and loading the page
  writes no setting. Every dynamic string is inserted through
  textContent - nothing a settings file could carry can inject
  markup - and "<" is escaped in the embedded JSON. The URL shape
  exists once (SettingsDiffPage::pageUrl / presetIdFromUrl), shared
  by the menu entries, the picker button and the page's own links.
  Preset provenance now travels to the import UIs: a file saved from
  a preset carries the "preset" field, the porter's Preview reports
  it verbatim (whether it is one of the built-ins is the preset
  catalog's judgment, not the porter's), and the import preview
  dialog names it - resolved to the built-in's name with its
  description as tooltip when it is one of them, shown as the plain
  text it is when it is not, and no line at all when the file states
  no origin. A new offline verifier,
  tools/security/verify_settings_diff_page.py (46 checks, needs
  node), pins the page without a Qt build: the placeholder contract
  between the HTML and buildPage() (every token each side carries
  exists on the other), the no-innerHTML rule (the mock DOM throws
  on it), the URL shape on all three sides (page links, pageUrl(),
  the scheme handler registration), the data and i18n contracts
  between the page script and diffData()/i18nJson(), and the page
  script running in a Node VM for every shape diffData() can
  produce - overview, diff, no-change diff, unknown preset, broken
  preview. Two new autotests pin it in the build: the build test
  (every shape builds with every placeholder substituted, the
  overview counts equal the porter's preview counts, all seven
  sections accounted for as changed, skipped or already matching,
  and the URL round trips, whitespace trimmed) and the integration
  test (the real page through the real osza: scheme handler: the
  rendered cards, rows and summary lines equal what the porter
  computes for the same document, and the diff follows the
  settings - toggling fingerprint protection changes the essentials
  diff). The preview test pins the provenance propagation (a string
  carried verbatim, known or not; a non-string ignored), and the
  dialog test pins both provenance lines and that no provenance
  shows no line.
- The import preview's diff now says, in one line, which site
  exceptions an import would clear and which it would add. The raw
  row - "bank.example, shop.example" against "none" - asked the
  reader to diff two comma-joined lists in their head, exactly for
  the change whose consequences reach furthest: a cleared HTTPS-Only
  exception means the site is held to https from the next load on, a
  cleared fingerprint exception means randomization starts applying
  to it, and a preset (every baseline resets the exception lists to
  empty) hits every excluded site at once. The preview now computes
  the entries the import takes away and the entries it newly adds
  (compared case-insensitively, the way both tools match hosts, so
  an entry that only changes case is neither cleared nor added; a
  duplicate is reported once), carries them as structured data next
  to the display strings, and the dialog shows them as one-line
  summaries under the field - "Clears the exception for: ...",
  "Adds an exception for: ..." - computed and translated in the
  porter, so every preview UI says the same thing. The offline
  counterpart grew the same lines:
  verify_settings_porter.py --diff now prints the cleared and added
  sites under a changed exception list, and its self-test (59
  checks) pins the semantics for both directions, the
  deduplication, and the case-only non-change. The autotests pin
  the structured payload, the summary lines, and the dialog rows.
- Security tools (12): informed imports - a per-tool diff preview
  before anything is applied, and preset export from the picker.
  Importing a settings file is no longer a blind yes: after picking
  the file, a preview dialog shows what it would change in this
  profile, per tool and per field - the current live value against
  the value after the import - plus the tools the file does not
  mention (left unchanged) and the sections that will be skipped,
  with the reason the import would give. A mismatching checksum is
  called out in the same dialog and turns the confirmation button
  into an explicit "Import anyway" (with Cancel as the safe
  default), so the decision to import a file that was changed after
  its export is made with the diff in view. The preview is computed
  by the very same validation the import applies - the per-tool
  checks now live in one place (buildPlans) that both the apply
  step and the preview consume, so a preview can never drift from
  what the import actually does - and it reads the current values
  through the same live-manager snapshot an export uses. The
  preview itself never touches a setting. The fingerprint master
  seed stays out of it, exactly as it stays out of exports. The
  same dialog now also replaces the old question-box flow in both
  places an import starts: Preferences > Privacy > Security tools
  and the security dashboard's "Import / Export" menu. The offline
  counterpart for hand edits is
  tools/security/verify_settings_porter.py --diff FILE [AGAINST],
  which prints the same per-tool diff against another settings file
  or one of the built-in presets - the presets are rebuilt by
  parsing securitypresets.cpp, so the offline diff is against what
  the browser actually recommends; the verifier self-test pins the
  parser and the diff semantics (a missing exclusions field counts
  as the empty list the import resets to, a missing blocklist
  source counts as not set). The second half: a baseline can be
  handed to another machine or profile without first applying it
  here - the preset picker saves any of the three presets as a
  regular settings file ("Save to file..." next to the picker in
  Preferences, "Save baseline to file..." in the dashboard's
  "Import / Export" menu). The saved file is a first-class export
  of the preset's document: fresh exportedAt stamp, fresh
  checksum, tamper-evident - so it imports anywhere without the
  mismatch warning - and it keeps the "preset" provenance field,
  so a re-exported baseline still names itself. Three new
  autotests pin it all: the preview (per-tool diffs, the
  no-mutation guarantee, agreement between the announced skips and
  the import result, checksum-mismatch previews, the shared
  display names), the preset export (format, provenance,
  checksum, byte-identical tools, clean import, atomic overwrite,
  and that a refused export never destroys an existing file), and
  the preview dialog (the diff tree, and that "Import anyway" is
  recorded only for an accepted mismatching checksum).
- Security tools (11): recommended-baseline presets and
  checksum-verified settings files. A hardened setup no longer
  requires clicking through seven toggles: the new preset picker
  offers three ready-made configurations - Essentials (the four
  request-path protections: AdBlock, HTTPS-Only mode,
  tracking-parameter stripping and malicious-site protection; secure
  DNS, fingerprint randomization and the Global Privacy Control
  signal off, for networks and sites where the deeper tools cause
  trouble), Recommended baseline (all seven protections on with the
  project defaults: secure DNS with fallback to the system resolver,
  the default blocklist source, no site exceptions) and Maximum
  protection (all seven on and secure DNS only, with no fallback).
  A preset is a document in the same osza-security-settings format
  the importer reads, applied through the very same validation and
  the same live manager APIs - there is no second, weaker code path.
  Applying always asks first, because a preset replaces the
  configuration of every tool (site exception lists are reset: a
  baseline starts from the recommended state). The picker lives in
  Preferences > Privacy > Security tools ("Recommended baseline"
  row) and in the security dashboard footer's "Import / Export"
  menu under "Apply recommended baseline". The fingerprint master
  seed stays out of presets, exactly as it stays out of exports.
  Settings files become tamper-evident: every export now carries a
  checksum - the SHA-256 of the canonical serialization of its
  tools section, so reformatting the file or reordering keys keeps
  it valid while any changed value breaks it. An import refuses a
  file whose checksum does not match (it was hand-edited or
  corrupted after the export) and offers an explicit "import
  anyway"; files without a checksum, from older versions or
  deliberate hand edits, import as before. After an intentional
  hand edit, tools/security/verify_settings_porter.py
  --update-checksum validates the file and writes a fresh checksum,
  so the edit imports without the mismatch warning; the offline
  verifier now checks checksums the same way the importer does and
  cross-checks the new constants (algorithm, preset ids) against
  the C++ sources. The default blocklist source and the default
  secure DNS server template live once now, as public accessors on
  ThreatBlockManager and DnsOverHttpsManager that the sidebar, the
  presets and the tests all share. Two new autotests pin the
  checksum rules (valid, tampered, malformed, missing, overridden;
  reformatting stays valid) and the full preset behavior (ids,
  ordering, pinned content, application through the managers, state
  restoration).
- Security tools (10): import and export of the security-tools
  settings. The configuration of all seven bundled protections -
  AdBlock, HTTPS-Only mode (with its exception list), tracking
  parameter stripping, secure DNS (mode and server templates),
  malicious site protection (switch and blocklist source),
  fingerprint protection (switch and exception list) and the Global
  Privacy Control signal - can now be saved to one portable JSON
  document and applied to another machine or profile, so a hardened
  setup can be backed up, moved or shared as a recommended baseline.
  New "Export..." / "Import..." buttons live in Preferences > Privacy >
  Security tools and in the security dashboard footer ("Import /
  Export"). Exports are written atomically (a failed export never
  destroys an existing backup) from the live manager state, not stale
  keys. Imports validate every tool section on its own - a malformed
  or unsupported section is skipped with a reason, the rest still
  applies - and refuse files that are not Osza security settings or
  come from a newer format version. The fingerprint master seed is
  deliberately never part of the format: it is the per-profile secret
  that keeps every user's noise unique, and copying it between
  browsers would give two machines the identical fingerprint; the
  protection counters are usage history, not settings. Three new
  autotests pin the document structure (including the seed-privacy
  guarantee), a full file round-trip through every manager, and the
  validation rules; the offline
  tools/security/verify_settings_porter.py validates a settings file
  without a Qt build and cross-checks the format constants against
  the C++ source, so hand-edited files can be checked before they are
  imported.
- Security tools (9): the security self-test page, osza:selftest. The
  security suite now proves itself: the page measures its own canvas
  2D, WebGL and audio readouts through the very hooks a fingerprinting
  site would use - with digests, generic GPU strings and the classic
  OfflineAudioContext value shown live - verifies the readouts are
  stable across reloads, and grades every check (pass / attention /
  failed). The invisible per-site noise is made visible: a live tile
  shows the +-1 pattern applied to this very page, next to the
  computed patterns of two example hosts, so "different site, different
  pattern; same site, same pattern" can be seen instead of believed.
  The page also lists the on/off state of all seven protections as of
  the moment it was loaded, checks the Global Privacy Control signal,
  and links to EFF Cover Your Tracks and BrowserLeaks for verification
  on a real site. Reachable from the new "Security self-test" button
  in the security dashboard sidebar, or by typing osza:selftest. The
  digests are produced by a self-contained SHA-256 in the page (no
  secure-context dependency), verified against the published test
  vectors. FingerprintManager gains a C++ mirror of the guard's
  perturbation (siteNoisePattern) that computes the demo tiles; the
  in-browser selfTestNoiseEquivalenceTest autotest pins it to the
  script's actual pixel deviations, and the offline
  tools/security/verify_fingerprint_guard.py (extended) and the new
  tools/security/verify_selftest_page.py check both without a Qt
  build. The offline page verifier is what caught a wrong constant in
  the page's SHA-256 before it ever shipped.

Fixed:
- Three latent bugs in the security autotests, none of which had
  ever executed anywhere: the fast build check only compiles them,
  and the local builds had been broken for the same stretch, so the
  suite's first full run today was its first run at all. The padlock
  deep-link test crashed with a null view: it asked a freshly
  created window for the page it is showing, but a window builds
  its first tab in postLaunch(), deferred to the event loop, so
  there was no tab to ask about yet - the test now waits for one
  (QTRY_VERIFY). The porter document test demanded an "enabled"
  field from all seven tool sections, but secure DNS carries its
  mode instead - the mode is the enabled state there, 0 being
  Disabled, exactly as the format documentation spells it - so the
  test now asserts secure DNS by its own shape. And the diff page
  integration test compared the unknown-tool warning's diff against
  a preview computed before the test itself turned the fingerprint
  protection off, one change the page rightly no longer shows; the
  expected count is now recomputed from the profile as it stands.
  The whole suite passes: 42 checks, plus the neighbouring autotest
  binaries (adblock, qztools, sqldatabase, cookies, locationbar,
  opensearch, password backends) re-run green.
- The build was broken on every push since the settings-diff deep
  links landed, in two places the fast build check never got far
  enough to report at once. First, SecurityImportPreviewDialog's
  header declared its diffPageRequested signal through the bare
  "signals:" keyword - the only such spelling in the whole tree. The
  project builds through KDECompilerSettings, whose modern-code
  definitions include -DQT_NO_KEYWORDS: under it Qt defines only the
  always-available Q_SIGNALS spelling, so the AUTOMOC unity
  translation unit (mocs_compilation.cpp) failed with "'signals'
  does not name a type" right at the class. The declaration now uses
  Q_SIGNALS, like every other header; the keyword sweep found no
  other bare signals/slots/emit anywhere. Second - unmasked only
  once the first was fixed - WebPage::featurePermissionRequested
  switched on QWebEnginePage::ClipboardSanitizedWrite behind a
  "Qt 6.6 split" guard, but no Qt release ever shipped that
  enumerator: Qt maps both of Chromium's clipboard permissions,
  sanitized write and read-write, onto the one combined
  QWebEnginePage::ClipboardReadWrite feature (in every version from
  6.4 through 6.11, both through the legacy signal and through
  QWebEnginePermission), so the case compiled nowhere. The osza:
  clipboard answer is now the one combined feature, granted - the
  same trust the legacy execCommand write path already extends to
  the browser's own pages, whose content is all browser-shipped with
  dynamic strings rendered through textContent; a read cannot be
  told apart from a write by this API, and no page the browser
  ships reads the clipboard. The autotests had two masked errors of
  their own from the same stretch (they had not compiled since the
  import-diff commits): SecurityToolsTest used QLabel without
  including it, and passed a brace-initialized two-element
  QStringList straight into QCOMPARE, where the comma split the
  macro's arguments; the include is added and the list is
  parenthesized. The diff-page verifier's static clipboard pins
  follow the corrected policy (one combined feature, granted, no
  version guard, no reference to the enumerator Qt never had).
  Verified by a full local compile of every target including the
  autotests against Qt 6.8 with the same ECM definitions the CI
  uses, and by all four offline security verifiers.
- The browser crashed on every launch and no window ever appeared:
  MainApplication called OszaIcons::activateTheme() while still inside
  its constructor, before Settings::createSettings() had run, and
  activateTheme() dereferenced the not-yet-created global settings
  object (a guaranteed nullptr crash on every platform, fresh or
  existing profile). The call now happens after the settings exist and
  activateTheme() is null-safe on top, falling back to palette
  detection when called before them.

Added:
- Fingerprint-protection exceptions can now be managed in bulk, in
  Preferences > Privacy > Security tools > "Fingerprint exceptions"
  (new FingerprintExceptionsDialog, the counterpart of the HTTPS-Only
  exceptions dialog). Until now exclusions were only reachable one at
  a time, from the padlock menu of a loaded site; the dialog lists
  them all, adds hosts (an entry covers its subdomains; "*.domain"
  covers the subdomains only) and removes them, and accepting the
  dialog regenerates the guard script immediately so the change
  applies to newly loaded pages. A new autotest covers the dialog's
  round-trip and the settings path the preferences window uses.
- Security tools (8): the Global Privacy Control signal, on by default.
  Osza now speaks GPC - the successor of Do Not Track with actual
  legal weight - and it does so completely, in both places the
  specification demands: every outgoing request carries the
  "Sec-GPC: 1" opt-out header (navigations and subresources alike,
  set by the URL interceptor next to DNT, which remains available),
  and every frame reports the opt-out to page scripts through
  navigator.globalPrivacyControl, set before any page script runs
  (new GpcManager, installed through the same machinery as the
  fingerprint guard: document creation, main world, subframes, and
  removed entirely when the signal is off so pages see the plain
  browser environment). Both halves follow one preference -
  Preferences > Privacy > Security tools > "Send Global Privacy
  Control signal" - and the security dashboard gained a matching
  quick switch with a state line. Where the law recognizes the
  signal (California, Colorado, Connecticut and several other
  jurisdictions), sites must honor it as an opt-out from the sale or
  sharing of personal data; elsewhere it is already respected by the
  major privacy-aware sites. The DOM half is verified end-to-end by
  an autotest that loads a page and reads the property back, and the
  network half by one that captures the raw request on loopback and
  asserts the header is present when the signal is on and absent
  when it is off.
- Security tools (7): browser-fingerprint randomization, on by
  default. A small guard script is injected into every frame before
  any page script runs (new FingerprintManager, Preferences > Privacy
  > Security tools > "Randomize browser fingerprints") and makes the
  classic fingerprinting readouts unusable for tracking: canvas 2D
  (getImageData, toDataURL, toBlob, also on OffscreenCanvas), WebGL
  (readPixels, plus the UNMASKED_VENDOR/UNMASKED_RENDERER GPU
  strings, which are replaced with generic values) and audio
  (AnalyserNode frequency and time-domain data plus AudioBuffer
  channel readouts - the classic OfflineAudioContext fingerprint)
  get per-site noise,
  and navigator.hardwareConcurrency/deviceMemory report per-site
  common values instead of the real hardware. The noise is
  deterministic - it depends only on a per-profile master seed
  (generated on first use, kept in the profile), the site host and
  the pixel position - so readouts stay stable for one site across
  reloads (sites keep working; a fingerprint that changed on every
  load would itself be fingerprintable) while differing between
  sites, between profiles and between users. Every hook fails closed
  to the original behaviour, so pages never break. Sites that misbehave
  can be excluded per site from the padlock (site info) menu, which
  states the current state and offers one-click shortcuts like the
  HTTPS-Only ones; the security dashboard gained a "Fingerprint
  protection" quick switch with a state line that names the current
  site when it is excluded. The guard script is additionally verified
  standalone, without a Qt build, by tools/security/
  verify_fingerprint_guard.py (node-based, 44 behavioural checks).
- Security tools (3): DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH). All hostname resolution
  performed by the engine can now go through an encrypted DoH server
  instead of the system resolver, so the network operator can neither
  read nor modify the DNS answers (Preferences > Privacy > Security
  tools > Secure DNS). Two modes are available: secure with fallback
  to the system DNS, and secure-only without fallback; the server can
  be picked from the known providers (Cloudflare, Google, Quad9,
  AdGuard) or entered as a custom RFC 8484 URI template. The feature
  is wired through the new DnsOverHttpsManager on top of QtWebEngine's
  global DNS mode (QtWebEngine >= 6.6; on older engines the controls
  are disabled) and surfaces its current state in the security
  dashboard.
- Security tools (4): the security dashboard, a new built-in sidebar
  (View > Sidebar > Security, Ctrl+Shift+D, or Tools > Security
  Dashboard). It shows live counters for the whole suite - ads blocked
  by AdBlock, tracking parameters stripped and insecure connections
  upgraded to HTTPS - both as persisted totals and for the current
  site, with quick switches for every protection (AdBlock, HTTPS-Only,
  tracking-parameter stripping, secure DNS) and a reset button. The
  counters are collected in a new thread-safe SecurityManager hub
  (the interceptors run on the IO thread) and survive restarts.
- Security tools (6): malicious-site protection, the blocklist-driven
  counterpart to the per-request tools. Navigations to hosts on a
  curated security blocklist (default: the StevenBlack hosts list,
  downloaded in bulk from the configured source - no browsing data is
  ever sent) are stopped with a full-screen "Dangerous site blocked"
  interstitial (osza:threatblocked) styled like the other internal
  pages, and requests for frames, scripts and other subresources of
  listed hosts are blocked outright. The interstitial offers "Back to
  safety" and a clearly-marked "Proceed anyway (not recommended)"
  that whitelists the host for the current session only. Listed hosts
  cover their subdomains; the blocklist is refreshed every 12 hours
  (plus on demand from Preferences > Privacy > Security tools, where
  the source URL is also configurable and accepts hosts-file, plain
  host and URL-per-line feeds), is persisted in the profile so it
  keeps working offline, and honors a 1,000,000-entry safety cap
  against broken sources. The security dashboard gained a fourth
  counter ("Threats blocked", per site and in total) and a quick
  switch with an honest state line ("N hosts, updated ..." /
  "Blocklist not downloaded yet"), and a new online autotest
  (threatblockonlinetest, BUILD_TESTING_ONLINE) fails loudly if the
  default source ever goes away - which is exactly how the originally
  planned URLhaus default was discovered to be retired (410 Gone)
  and replaced with a live source before release.
- Security tools (5): per-site HTTPS-Only shortcuts in the padlock
  (site info) menu. The menu now states the HTTPS-Only state for the
  site ("HTTP connections to this site are upgraded / never upgraded")
  and offers one-click shortcuts: "Always upgrade to HTTPS" removes a
  site from the exception list, "Never upgrade to HTTPS" adds one, and
  when the mode is off it can be turned on right from the menu. A
  plain-http page reloads immediately after the change so the shortcut
  takes effect at once.
- Security tools (1): HTTPS-Only mode. When enabled (Preferences >
  Privacy > Security tools), every insecure http:// request is
  transparently upgraded to https:// by a new request interceptor -
  top-level navigations as well as subresources, before anything
  touches the network. Local addresses (localhost, loopback), private
  network ranges (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16, IPv6 ULA
  and link-local, .local and .localhost names) are never upgraded, and
  a per-site exception list ("HTTPS-Only exceptions" dialog) covers
  the servers that only exist over plain HTTP.
- Security tools (2): tracking-parameter stripping, on by default.
  Known click-identifier query parameters (utm_*, fbclid, gclid,
  msclkid, twclid, ttclid, _hsenc, s_kwcid and friends - 33 names) are
  removed from every http(s) request before it is sent, so links that
  were shared or pasted with campaign trackers load clean. Signed CDN
  URLs are untouched (they do not use these parameters) and the
  feature can be turned off next to HTTPS-Only mode.
Changed:
- Every icon in the product was redrawn as one original, coherent set
  (see ICONS.md for the system). The browser now ships a complete
  bundled "Osza" icon theme - a light and a dark variant covering all
  81 themed icon names the code requests, plus hand-tuned 16 px versions
  of the high-traffic toolbar glyphs - so no toolbar, menu, sidebar,
  preferences page or dialog shows blank or mismatched icons anymore,
  on any platform and in light or dark mode.
- Osza's UI icons no longer follow the desktop icon theme on Linux:
  the bundled theme is active on all platforms and the system theme is
  only a fallback for names Osza does not ship. The browser therefore
  looks identical on Linux, Windows and macOS. This is a deliberate
  behavior change; a restart is required after switching palettes,
  like the chrome themes.
- The tab close button is no longer a saturated red cross: a quiet
  neutral cross, a darker hover state and a red pressed confirmation,
  each legible on light and dark chrome. Toolbar hover overlays no
  longer use hard-coded black translucency (which was invisible on
  dark palettes) and follow the active palette instead.
- The internal pages' artwork (start page, about, restore, crash and
  the speed dial buttons), the preferences page icons, the site info
  dialog, the loading spinner and every plugin glyph were redrawn on
  the same system. The start page and about art now use the Osza ring
  and wordmark instead of leftover artwork from the project's origins.
- App and packaging art (hicolor set, Windows .ico, macOS .icns, DMG
  background, document-type icons, README banner) was regenerated from
  the brand mark, with a simplified variant for 32 px and below.

Removed:
- The "breeze-fallback" icon theme (17 hard-coded dark-grey icons),
  the unused legacy location bar PNGs, the unused third-party speed
  dial backgrounds and three orphaned mac theme images.

Added:
- tools/icons: a design system, a deterministic build pipeline and a
  static auditor for icons and resources (development tools only, no
  new build or runtime dependencies). ICONS.md documents the grid,
  palette, states and how to add an icon.

Fixed:
- The bookmark editor's speed dial button showed no icon at all: it
  referenced a resource that never existed (:/icons/theme/
  speeddial.png) and now uses a proper glyph.
- The Linux build check failed to compile: oszaicons.cpp called
  Settings::globalSettings()->value() without including <QSettings>,
  leaving the type incomplete (the -Werror=return-type error on top
  was a cascade of that).
- The README banner and the DMG background lost the bottom of the
  wordmark: the icon pipeline's svg_to_png() defaulted the output
  height to the width, which letterboxed the 494x132 brand lockup
  into a square canvas and pushed the wordmark below the banners'
  bottom edges. The README logo is now shown at 360 px so the mark
  and wordmark are legible.
- CI: the snap release job retries once on a clean managed instance
  before failing, because snapcraft's LXD build VM intermittently
  fails its initial apt update ("Failed to refresh package list").

1.2  (2026-11-01)
-----------------
The modern chrome refresh release.

Added:
- New bundled "Osza Cyber" theme (Preferences -> Themes) applying the
  design system of the internal pages to the browser chrome: rounded
  tab cards with a neon cyan top edge, dark pill-shaped address and
  search fields with cyan focus rings, dark menus with cyan-tinted
  hover states, a bundled dark icon set referenced directly so the
  dark chrome never gets light artwork, and a private-browsing
  variant that shifts the accent to violet (private.css).

Changed:
- The default Windows, macOS and Linux themes were modernized into a
  shared "modern neutral" chrome: one tinted canvas behind the tab
  strip, rounded selected-tab cards that connect to the toolbar band,
  flat toolbar buttons with quiet hover states, pill-shaped location
  and search fields with a teal focus ring matching the in-app accent,
  rounded menus, a separated status bar and larger hit areas for the
  tab close button and the strip controls. Every color resolves from
  the active palette, so the chrome follows the system light and dark
  scheme automatically. The macOS theme now resolves icons through
  the theme icon mechanism (with the bundled fallbacks) instead of
  the legacy four-state PNG artwork.

Fixed:
- CI: the KDE Neon apt repository is now fetched from the CDN-backed
  archive.neon.kde.org endpoint over HTTPS instead of the single
  origin.archive.neon.kde.org backend server, which intermittently
  refuses connections and broke the Linux build check on 2026-10-31
  (the two endpoints serve a byte-identical archive). The snap recipe
  points at the same endpoint, the workflows fall back to the origin
  server only if the CDN cannot be reached, and the dependency
  installer now passes apt retry options so transient network blips
  no longer abort a run.

0.1.1  (2026-10-27)
-------------------
First release published under the Osza Browser identity.

Changed:
- Complete rename of every part of the project from Veex to Osza:
  namespace, classes, identifiers, files, build targets, icons,
  translation catalogs and application identity.
- New application identity: org.osza.OszaBrowser, executable "osza".
- QML module URI is now org.osza; Python plugin symbols are now
  pyosza_load_plugin / pyosza_init_plugin.
- Desktop, appstream, snap, flatpak, AppImage and CI packaging updated
  to the new names, paths and osza-tools GitHub links.
- New brand identity "Orbit": the lowercase o of osza drawn as a bold
  open ring with an orbiting comet dot on a deep-space squircle, in a
  mint-cyan-indigo gradient anchored on the in-app accent (#00ffd1).
  Includes the full "osza" wordmark (Quicksand Bold letterforms) used
  by the start page and the about dialog with proper light/dark logo
  variants, flat black/white emblem variants, complete icon sets
  (hicolor, Windows .ico, macOS .icns) and the DMG installer icon,
  replacing the temporary gold medallion.

Fixed:
- Release CI: Windows packages are now built against Qt 6.8 LTS with
  the MSVC 2022 archives instead of Qt 6.7 (MSVC 2019), pulling in the
  much newer Chromium that the current 6.8 patch releases bundle.
- Release CI: macOS packaging no longer aborts in the bundle step by
  writing a multi-line value (find output) to GITHUB_ENV, which GitHub
  Actions rejects; the unused variable was removed.
- Build: define REQUIRED_QT_VERSION so the Qt >= 6.9 Qt6NetworkPrivate
  lookup gets a proper version constraint instead of an empty variable.
- Release CI: fetch extra-cmake-modules and KF6 Archive from the KDE
  GitHub mirrors on Windows; the download.kde.org CDN stalls caused
  hour-long curl retry loops on the release runners.
- CI: new "Build check" workflow that compiles the tree (including
  autotests and benchmark targets) on every push to main and on pull
  requests, so build breakage no longer waits for a release tag.

0.1.0  (2026-10-23)
-------------------
First public release of Osza Browser.

Changed:
- Rebranded from Falkon (KDE) with a deep rename of the codebase:
  namespace, classes, identifiers, files, build targets and catalogs.
- New application identity: org.osza.OszaBrowser, executable "osza".
- New temporary logo and icon set (gradient V on dark diamond).
- Replaced upstream KDE service links with the Osza Tools GitHub project.
- Default bookmarks and speed dial now point to Osza Tools resources.
- Renamed the KDE Frameworks Integration plugin to System Integration.
- Removed KDE CI configuration files.

Fixed:
- Speed Dial thumbnails are now written atomically (encode to memory,
  commit via QSaveFile) so an interrupted save can no longer leave a
  truncated PNG on disk.
- Page thumbnailer loads its QML with the canonical qrc:/ resource URL.

Packaging:
- Cross-platform release CI: Linux tar.gz, Linux snap, macOS dmg and
  Windows zip are built and attached to every v* tag release.
- Added BUILD_TRANSLATIONS build option so platforms without Ki18n
  (e.g. Windows) can skip the po-installation requirement.
- Repository-local dependency installer: linux/install-build-deps.sh.

Notes:
- Osza Browser is based on Falkon, licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later.
- Original Falkon authors: David Rosca, Juraj Oravec and all
  Falkon/KDE contributors - thank you.
